REGIONAL ADVOCATE MODEL: HANDOFF DOCUMENT FOR ACADEMIC VALIDATION WHITE PAPER

TARGET AUDIENCE & GOAL
- Audience: Academics (peer review credibility)
- Purpose: Validate model so a legislator can propose a pilot study
- Legislator constraint: Must have funds to cover ~150K underserved people
- Success metric: Academic credibility > political palatability

CORE MECHANISM
- Region: 20,000 people (base unit; pilot ~7-8 regions for 150K)
- Advocates (former insurers, actuaries, clinics, health networks, etc.) bid via reverse Vickrey auction
  * Bid = 100% fixed rate + expected bonus value (50%)
  * Winner: lowest total bid; paid expected fixed rate plus expanded bonus to match second-lowest bid's expectation value
  * Tenure: Year 1 fixed + bonus (12-month); Year 2+ same, inflation-adjusted
  * Healthy regions: automatic continuation option
  * Unhealthy regions: re-bid annually
- Care transfer: Incoming Advocates assume all patients (no opt-out)
- Sanctions: Bottom 1/5 annual ranking OR >50% region loss → bars new bidding
  * Losing one region → can't bid next auction (unless no other bidders)
- Anti-monopoly: No Advocate >1/10th of all regions
- Sabotage prevention: Final month operating check tendered upon
  complete patient handoff certification

ENTITIES & ROLES
- Government: Collects taxes, pays rates/bonuses, sets/publishes metrics (6+ months
  advance), provides outcome data, manages waivers
- Auction Houses (profit/non-profit; operate nationally, not regionally):
  * Primary: 2-sided data marketplace (sell to researchers, Advocates, competitors)
  * Secondary: Hold anonymized bids, manage blinded researchers, validate via
    billing codes/interviews/registries
  * Compete on fee structure (e.g., 2% on Advocate bids, 0.5% on patient record
    access, subscription models)
  * Universities likely candidates (brand, program development, researcher access)
  * Can sell population health research for Advocates to purchase
- Advocates: Deliver/coordinate care, earn bonuses, manage copays (structure TBD),
  no mid-year transfers, recruit from competitive bidders motivated by profit
- Researchers: Blinded to Advocate/buyer identity, assigned by Auction Houses,
  validate via billing/registries, portable across Auction Houses
- Patients: Auto-transfer to new Advocates, can seek external care at own expense,
  sandboxed record access via Auction Houses

BONUS METRICS (Government decides unilaterally; public before year of effect)
- Primary (10): Disability-Free Life Expectancy, Age First Chronic Disease,
  Employment Rate, Cost/Healthy Life Year, Time to Hospitalization,
  Mortality (age-adj), 30-day Readmission, Functional Independence, Healthcare
  Utilization Efficiency, Patient-Reported Health Status
- Disparity (10, race-neutral statute; demographic tracking by Auction Houses):
  Maternal mortality/morbidity, Female AMI, Diabetes control, Opioid
  overdose/treatment, Colorectal cancer screening/mortality, Hypertension control,
  Breast cancer screening/mortality, Kidney disease, Mental health/suicide,
  Prostate cancer screening/mortality
- Data sources: Claims, pharmacy, registries, employment, hospital/ED, functional
  assessments, surveys, certificates
- Single composite score (prevent Goodharting; well-designed metrics are government
  responsibility; market prices in historical performance)
- No need for explicit causality attribution (metrics lag inherently)

ECONOMIC LOGIC
- Demand shift incentive: Prevention focus expected to shift utilization 15-25%
  (Kaiser benchmark: 15-25% leftward demand shift, 6-8 years longer life expectancy,
  50-70% more Stage 1 cancer diagnoses)
- Advocates bid lower fixed/higher bonus in affluent areas; higher fixed in poorer
  areas; rational Advocates build infrastructure (clinic, gym, grocery) in
  underserved areas
- Cost-effectiveness thresholds: UK £20-30K/QALY, AU A$50K/QALY, US $100-150K/QALY
- International convergence hypothesis: Price-competitive care + outcome incentives
  will gradually lower US medical spending toward international norms (offsetting
  drug R&D export revenue)
- Bundled payment precedent (BPCI, CJR): 56-60% cost/quality gains; use as
  estimation tool, not as logical extension claim

PILOT CRITERIA
- Legislator has funds to cover ~150K underserved people
- Target regions: Population 15K-50K each (underserved, no current Advocate
  presence, <2 PCPs/10K, Uninsured+Medicaid >40%)
- Geography: Defer to legislator/SBA on specific regions (Wyoming ≠ Baltimore;
  implementation details vary)
- Testable assumptions (model breaks if any fail):
  * Advocates can be recruited (and won't cherry-pick)
  * Bonuses shift behavior
  * Metrics are measurable and resistant to gaming
  * Auction Houses operate competitively
  * Patient transfer is operationally feasible

DECISIONS LOCKED IN
- Doctors maintain independent records
- Researchers blinded to Advocate/buyer identity
- No secondary mid-year transfers
- No patient opt-out on transfer
- Metrics announced 6+ months before effect
- Auction Houses compete nationally on fee structure
- Copays: Advocate responsibility (structure TBD)
- Re-bid logistics: Address as goals with explicit reasoning (TBD)
- Metrics governance: Government unilateral with input; Auction Houses sell stats
  for next-year metric improvement (not lobbying/model change)

OPEN FOR ACADEMIC INPUT
- Which international healthcare systems best serve as estimation benchmarks?
- How to project cost convergence toward international norms (with uncertainty bands)?
- Control region design (traditional FFS) feasible or infeasible for credibility?
- Metrics validation: What external data triangulation prevents gaming?
- Workforce implications: Displaced specialist support; geographic planning?
- Re-bid logistics: Explicit notice periods, appeal rights, 90-day transition?
- Metrics governance detail: Process for mid-year Auction House metric proposals?
- SBA budget & qualifying region criteria: Academic to guide/defer to legislator

